<DocsLayout title="KOSync-compatible apps" description="Sync reading positions with Readest and KOReader's built-in progress sync — no plugin required.">
  <SectionHero section="integrations" />
  <h1>KOSync-compatible apps</h1>
  <p>
    Tome speaks the KOSync protocol — the sync API popularised by KOReader's built-in <strong>Progress sync</strong> and supported by third-party readers like <a href="https://github.com/readest/readest" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Readest</a>.
    Point any of them at your server and reading positions follow you between those apps <em>and</em> from Tome's web reader.
  </p>
  <DocsMeta />


  <Callout variant="info" title="Using KOReader? The plugin does more">
    On KOReader itself, <a href={withBase("/docs/koreader")}>the TomeSync plugin</a> is the
    full experience: exact position sync, session recording for stats, series downloads,
    highlight sync. The KOSync endpoint is for everything that <em>isn't</em> running the
    plugin — Readest on your phone or desktop, or a KOReader setup you'd rather keep stock.
  </Callout>

  <h2>Setup</h2>
  <Steps>
    <li>In Tome, open <strong>Settings → KOReader</strong> and set a sync password under <strong>KOSync</strong>. This creates your sync account (same username as your Tome login).</li>
    <li>In the client, configure a custom sync server pointing at <code>https://your-server/api/v1</code>.
      In KOReader that's <strong>Tools → Progress sync → Custom sync server</strong>;
      in Readest it's under <strong>Settings → Sync</strong>.</li>
    <li>Log in with your Tome username and the sync password. Done — reading a book pushes
      progress on close, and opening it pulls the latest position.</li>
  </Steps>

  <h2>What syncs</h2>
  <p>
    Positions sync at <strong>percentage precision</strong> across app families. Two KOReader-family
    clients exchange exact locators as usual, but the web reader speaks EPUB CFI and KOSync
    clients don't — the formats aren't convertible, so cross-family sync opens the book at the
    right percentage rather than the exact paragraph.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>From Tome to your app:</strong> the sync pull answers with whatever is newest —
      the last KOSync push, the web reader's position, or a TomeSync device's position. Read a
      few chapters in the browser at lunch and Readest picks up where you stopped.</li>
    <li><strong>From your app to Tome:</strong> pushes update the book's progress and read
      status (books finish at 99%, and a finished book stays finished on a re-read). They
      deliberately do <em>not</em> move positions on devices running the TomeSync plugin —
      third-party clients can't disturb your e-reader's sync.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Linking synced books</h2>
  <p>
    KOSync identifies a book by a hash of the file, so Tome has to connect that hash to the
    right library entry before progress can flow. Any file that was downloaded from Tome —
    web download, OPDS, or the plugin — is recognised automatically by its hash on the first
    push or pull. Nothing to do.
  </p>
  <p>
    For a file Tome has never served (sideloaded from elsewhere), the book page offers a
    manual picker: it lists synced documents that aren't attached to any book yet, with
    device, progress and date, and one click links the right one.
  </p>

  <h3>Sideloaded books that name themselves (experimental)</h3>
  <p>
    Some clients can send the file's title and author along with the position. CrossInk
    calls the switch <strong>Send Document Metadata</strong>. With that on, Tome needs no
    manual link:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The book is in your library:</strong> if exactly one book you can see has
      the same title and the same authors, the file is linked to it. A shared ISBN also
      counts when the title or the author agrees.</li>
    <li><strong>The book is not in your library:</strong> Tome adds it without a file, so
      its progress and reading show on Home, in the stats and on the calendar under its
      real title. It shows in your own Library and in library search with a
      <strong>No file</strong> badge, has a page of its own for a cover and details, never
      goes into a library, and stays out of series pages, OPDS, the plugin and the apps,
      because there is nothing to download. Only you see it.</li>
    <li><strong>Several books fit:</strong> Tome does not choose. Use the manual picker;
      it shows the title the reader sent.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    When you add the real file later, open its page and use <em>Link KOReader sync</em>:
    the reading, read status, finish date and rating move over and the file-less entry is
    removed. Deleting a file-less book tells Tome to stop tracking that file; linking the
    document by hand brings it back.
  </p>
  <p>
    Matching is exact, never by similarity. Case, spacing and the order of several authors
    do not matter; the volume does. A reader that says "volume 2", or a file named like
    volume 2, is not linked to a book Tome has as volume 1, even when both carry the bare
    series name as their title. A title without an author is never matched.
  </p>

  <div class="docs-shot">
    <ThemedShot client:load name="kosync-link" alt="The Link KOReader sync picker on a book page, offering an unlinked Readest document" />
  </div>

  <h2>Reading stats from CrossInk (experimental)</h2>
  <p>
    KOSync itself only carries a position, so most KOSync apps add nothing to your stats.
    <a href="https://github.com/uxjulia/CrossInk" target="_blank" rel="noopener">CrossInk</a>,
    the firmware for Xteink readers, is the exception from version 2.0: it can also send its
    reading sessions, and Tome accepts them. Each sitting arrives with its book, start, end
    and active reading time, and counts in the stats pages, the calendar and streaks.
  </p>
  <Steps>
    <li>Set the sync password in <strong>Settings → KOReader</strong> if you haven't. An account
      registered from the reader itself is not tied to a Tome user and gets no stats.</li>
    <li>On the reader, <strong>Authenticate</strong> against your Tome server again. That is
      when it checks what the server supports.</li>
    <li>Turn on <strong>Reading Stats</strong> under <strong>What to Sync</strong>, then sync.
      <strong>Clippings</strong> can be switched on there too (see below).</li>
  </Steps>
  <ul>
    <li>The reader syncs with one server. While it points at Tome, it sends nothing to
      CrossPoint Sync, and reading it already sent there is not sent to Tome.</li>
    <li>Sessions arrive with time but no page counts. The reader sends a page total per
      book instead, and Tome turns that into pages per session: the counter's growth between
      two syncs is shared out over the sessions read in between, by reading time, marked with
      a small sign as attributed. Sync after each sitting and every session gets its exact
      pages; sync less often and a larger total is spread over more sessions. A book's
      all-time <strong>Pages</strong> and <strong>Pace</strong> always match the reader's
      counter. Reading from before Tome saw the counter, and history from the import, stays
      as time only.</li>
    <li>On CrossInk 2.0.0-rc, <strong>Sync Reading Stats</strong> for more than one book at a
      time reports a failure with any server. The data still arrives; syncing from inside a
      book works.</li>
    <li>A session for a book Tome can't identify still counts toward your totals and is listed
      as "(book not linked yet)". It appears on the calendar once the document is linked:
      automatically when the reader next syncs progress for a book you downloaded from Tome
      or, with <strong>Send Document Metadata</strong> on, for any book (see
      <em>Sideloaded books that name themselves</em> above), or by hand with
      <em>Link KOReader sync</em> on the book's page.</li>
    <li>A session you trim or delete in Tome stays that way, even when the reader sends it again.</li>
  </ul>

  <h3>Clippings (experimental)</h3>
  <p>
    A clipping is a passage you save on the reader. With <strong>Clippings</strong> switched on
    under <strong>What to Sync</strong>, the reader uploads its clippings to Tome and restores
    the ones made on your other CrossInk readers; a clipping deleted on one reader is removed
    everywhere. In Tome they appear on the <strong>Highlights</strong> page and in the book's
    <em>Highlights and notes</em>, marked as CrossInk clippings, as soon as the file is linked
    to a book. They are read-only here: change or delete them on the reader. They are not sent
    to KOReader devices and the iOS app does not show them yet.
  </p>

  <h2>Troubleshooting</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>"401 Unauthorized"</strong> — the sync password doesn't match. Set it again in <strong>Settings → KOReader</strong>; re-saving overwrites the old credential.</li>
    <li><strong>Progress lands in the app but not in Tome</strong> — the document isn't linked
      to a book yet. Open the book's page and use <em>Link KOReader sync</em>.</li>
    <li><strong>The app doesn't jump to the web position</strong> — some clients only apply a
      pulled position when it's ahead of the local one, or ask before jumping backwards.
      Check the client's conflict setting (KOReader: <strong>Progress sync → Sync behaviour</strong>).</li>
    <li><strong>CrossInk shows stats as "Not supported"</strong> — it remembers the answer from
      the last time you authenticated. Set the sync password in <strong>Settings → KOReader</strong>,
      make sure the server is on a Tome version with stats support, then authenticate on the
      reader again.</li>
  </ul>
</DocsLayout>
